course: dictionary
lesson: 9064
title: Small changes with slightly
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word slightly means a little bit, not very much. When you see it, ask yourself: is this a small change or a big one? «The door was slightly open.» The door is not wide open, only a small gap. Swap in «a little bit» and the sentence still makes sense, so slightly means a little.
explain: The words around slightly give clues. «A 2022 survey counted about 1,250 finless porpoises, slightly more than five years before.» The word more tells you the number went up, and slightly tells you it went up only a little. Ask: is the difference big or small? Slightly always means small.
explain: Slightly can also describe size or shape. «Slightly built» means small and thin, like a small, slim person. Here slightly does not mean a little bit of a change over time, but a little bit of a quality. Look at what the word is describing, then choose the meaning that fits.
fact: Slightly means a little bit, not very much.
fact: «Slightly» can mean in a small or thin way, as in «slightly built» for a small, thin person.
fact: «Slightly more» and «slightly less» show a small difference between two amounts.
fact: «Slightly late» sounds softer than «very late».
fact: A good check is to swap in «a little bit»: if the sentence still makes sense, slightly is right.
exercise: «Taking vitamin C every day might shorten a cold slightly in some studies.» What does slightly mean here? | Completely, all the way | Never at all | *A little, not by much | Very quickly
exercise: What does «slightly built» describe? | A fast car | A very loud person | A tall, strong person | *A small, thin person
exercise: Find and copy the one word that tells you only a small amount: «The door was slightly open.» | was | *slightly | door | open
test: «Rainwater is slightly acidic and slowly dissolves rocks such as limestone.» What does slightly acidic suggest about rainwater? | Frozen solid | Sweet like sugar | *A little acidic, not very strong | Very, very strong acid
test: Which is the softer way to say «very late»? | Late forever | Extremely late | *Slightly late | Very late
exam: Mum says, «The soup is slightly too salty.» What does she mean? | Not salty at all | *Salty, but only a little | Sweet like sugar | Extremely salty, nobody can eat it
